Make touch -d and -t follow a change to TZ made in the shell.
The Microsoft C runtime reads TZ at the first time conversion in a
process and never again. touch is a NOFORK applet, so in the shell it
runs in a process that may already have converted a time: after one
touch -d, a later TZ=JST-9 touch -d, or an export of a new TZ, still
read the time in the old zone.

Call tzset() before converting the time, so the runtime reads TZ anew.

---
 coreutils/touch.c |    5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

--- a/coreutils/touch.c
+++ b/coreutils/touch.c
@@ -142,6 +142,11 @@
 		time_t t;
 		int check_dst;
 
+#if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+		/* The C runtime reads TZ only once, but touch is NOFORK:
+		 * the shell may have changed TZ since then. */
+		tzset();
+#endif
 		//memset(&tm_time, 0, sizeof(tm_time));
 		/* Better than memset: makes "HH:MM" dates meaningful */
 		time(&t);
